---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const roles = [
  {
    when: '2025 —',
    role: 'Software Engineer',
    org: 'Helply',
    detail:
      'Rebuilding a customer-support platform that has been running for over a decade, in NestJS and SQL. I own the email-rendering pipeline and the notes architecture. The volume is manageable: organisations arrive with millions of tickets, each carrying thousands of emails, attachments and notes. The history is the hard part. Every migration has to keep the old schema and the new one agreeing while both are serving live traffic. I also built the video sync, editing and rendering, down to manipulating individual segments on the timeline.',
  },
  {
    when: '2023 — 2025',
    role: 'Founding Engineer',
    org: 'Scream Creator Technologies',
    detail:
      'Three engineers, two designers, and three products taken from zero to paying customers. I built the video pipeline end to end: capture, re-encode of camera and screen, quality enhancement, proxy renditions, an editor with TTS voiceover synced through FFmpeg, then export. Export ran on Remotion Lambda for a year and eight months, fanned out in eight-to-ten-minute sections to keep clear of Lambda’s fifteen-minute ceiling, with per-render cost tracked for the whole of it. The obvious next step was to build our own renderer; the cost that decided against it was three engineers’ attention, not the AWS bill. Then customers told us they were waiting on sharing rather than on renders, which made render speed the wrong thing to buy at any price. We shipped a desktop app instead and moved rendering onto the user’s machine with FFmpeg and Remotion running locally. I also wired Stripe and Razorpay across the whole payment lifecycle: subscriptions, GST invoicing, webhooks, refunds, retries, disputes.',
  },
  {
    when: '2023',
    role: 'Engineering Intern',
    org: 'Scream Creator Technologies',
    detail:
      'Replaced a third-party email vendor with an event-driven pipeline on SES and Lambda, which meant taking on bounces, suppression and retries ourselves. Built the event pipeline behind product analytics. Took public-page Lighthouse scores from 68 to 95 with code-splitting and lazy-loaded images.',
  },
  {
    when: '2019 — 2023',
    role: 'B.Tech, Computer Science & Engineering',
    org: 'Tezpur University',
    detail: 'CGPA 8.09 / 10.',
  },
];

const projects = [
  {
    name: 'Manthan',
    href: 'https://github.com/Manthan933/Manthan',
    stack: 'React · Node · Express · MongoDB',
    detail:
      'A proctored online exam platform with webcam monitoring and plagiarism detection, built from scratch and used by three institutes and around 350 students. I open-sourced it once it worked; 41 contributors and 145 forks have arrived since.',
  },
  {
    name: 'DETR, reimplemented',
    href: 'https://github.com/aavishkarmishra/detr-tensorflow',
    stack: 'TensorFlow · Keras',
    detail:
      'End-to-End Object Detection with Transformers, rebuilt from the paper in TensorFlow, with web inference through OpenCV. I never trained it to convergence — the paper takes 300 epochs on eight GPUs, which I did not have — so this is a study of how set prediction and the bipartite matching loss work, not a reproduction of its numbers.',
  },
];

const stack = [
  'TypeScript', 'NestJS', 'Node', 'Python', 'FastAPI', 'React',
  'SQL / Postgres', 'Redis', 'MongoDB',
  'AWS — Lambda, SES, S3, EC2, CloudFront', 'Linux', 'GitHub Actions',
  'FFmpeg', 'TensorFlow',
];
---
<Base title="Work" description="Experience, projects, and the systems I have owned end to end.">
  <h1>Work</h1>
  <p class="dim lede-sub">
    Three years of building products, most of it as the person who also
    carried the pager for them.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">Experience</p>
  <ul class="timeline">
    {roles.map((r) => (
      <li>
        <span class="when nums">{r.when}</span>
        <div class="what">
          <div class="role">{r.role}<span class="org">, {r.org}</span></div>
          <p class="detail">{r.detail}</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p class="eyebrow spaced">How I work</p>
  <ul class="how">
    <li>I price a design before I commit to it, and the price includes our own
      time. Building our own renderer was the interesting option and the wrong
      one. Three engineers cannot afford to maintain infrastructure that moving
      the work to the client makes unnecessary.</li>
    <li>On a system with history, the migration is the feature. Two schemas
      serving live traffic and agreeing with each other is most of the work.
      None of it shows up in the demo.</li>
    <li>I read the platform limits before designing around them. Lambda caps
      execution at fifteen minutes. That one number decided the shape of our
      whole export pipeline.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="eyebrow spaced">Projects</p>
  <ul class="entries">
    {projects.map((p) => (
      <li>
        <a class="entry-title" href={p.href}>{p.name}</a>
        <p class="entry-desc">{p.detail}</p>
        <span class="entry-meta">{p.stack}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <p class="eyebrow spaced">Tools I reach for</p>
  <p class="stack">{stack.join('  ·  ')}</p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede-sub { font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0.5rem 0 2.25rem; }
  .eyebrow.spaced { margin-top: 3.25rem; }
  .how { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .how li {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    position: relative;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .how li + li { margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .how li::before {
    content: "—";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--faint);
  }
  .entries .entry-meta { display: block; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
  .stack { font: 400 0.875rem/1.9 var(--sans); color: var(--muted); }
</style>
